  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Noturus eleutherus Jordan, 1877. Common name: Mountain Madtom. Size: 13 cm (5.1\") SL. Robust body with a fairly deep caudal peduncle and a dark brown bar at the base of the caudal fin. The pectoral spine is long, with 6-10 prominent sawlike teeth on the rear edge and large teeth on the front edge. Brown or gray above, usually with dark mottling and usually 4 vague saddles; light below.</p><p>This species is highly endangered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>North America: Ohio River basin from northwestern Pennsylvania to eastern Illinois, and south to Tennessee and northern Georgia in the USA; White and St. Francis River drainages in Missouri and Arkansas; Mississippi River in western Tennessee; Ouachita River and Red River drainages in the USA.</p><p>Type locality: Big Pigeon River, tributary of French Broad River, Tennessee, U.S.A.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Occurs in clear, cool, rocky riffles, runs and flowing pools of creeks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Temperature: 14-22°C (57.2-71.6°F). pH 6.2-7.0. Highly oxygenated water is a must.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Provide sand mixed with peat moss, PVC pipes, flat rocks and vegetation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Readily feeds on live and frozen foods. Will also adapt to flake and prepared foods for the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Suitable for a coldwater community tank. Ideal tankmates include small to midsized North American native fishes such as darters and shiners.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Breeding male with swollen lips, enlarged cephalic epaxial muscles, and a large bulbous genital papilla. Female with a papilla with a deep median groove ending in a small bump.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Spawning occurs in the months of May-August. It is difficult in the aquarium, however it has been done when wild-caught specimens are added to aquariums with warmer temperatures than the river or drainage system they were captured from. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1078",
      "html": "<p>Annals of the Lyceum of Natural History of New York v. 11 (nos 11-12) (art. 29), pp 371.</p>"
    }
  ],
